Given the set $\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\}$ with a median of 9, we know that the middle number in this ordered set is the median. Since there are 5 numbers in the set, the median is the third number. Therefore, $n + 6 = 9$.

Solving for $n$:
\begin{align*}
n + 6 &= 9 \\
n &= 9 - 6 \\
n &= 3
\end{align*}

Now we substitute $n$ with 3 to find the set:
\begin{align*}
\{3, 3 + 5, 3 + 6, 3 + 9, 3 + 15\} &= \{3, 8, 9, 12, 18\}
\end{align*}

The mean is calculated by summing all the numbers in the set and dividing by the number of elements in the set. Thus, we calculate the mean as follows:
\begin{align*}
\text{Mean} &= \frac{3 + 8 + 9 + 12 + 18}{5} \\
&= \frac{50}{5} \\
&= 10
\end{align*}

Therefore, the mean of the set is $\boxed{10}$.
